#~ msgid "SOYPELUSA SUITS FIND NEW AMMO:  Following the infamous disaster of the DaiZoom product launch and the numerous lawsuits that sprung from its failure, research from Harvard's Cognitive Neuropsychology labs has brought new evidence in the ongoing litigation against the company.  We spoke with lead researcher Doctor Soren Eldridge who had this to say: \"The DaiZoom bar, beyond its notoriously bad taste, appears to cause in its consumers symptoms similar to those of a major depressive disorder.  There is an actual tangible impact on mood that lasts well beyond the initial consumption of even just a few bars in a short span of time - we suspect DaiZoom may actually be chemically inhibiting dopamine receptors.  This is unprecedented, and quite exciting, given that it consists entirely of otherwise biochemically bland ingredients.\"  Doctor Eldridge stated that further research would be needed to determine the full extent of the issue and whether consumption of DaiZoom had long-term health effects similar to existing dopamine blocking drugs, and if there is any role for it as a medical therapy in the future.  The full interview can be found on page 7."
#~ msgstr "SOYPELUSA诉讼找到了新的弹药：在DaiZoom产品发布的臭名昭著的灾难以及因其失败而引发的众多诉讼之后，哈佛大学认知神经心理学实验室的研究在针对该公司的持续诉讼中带来了新的证据。我们采访了首席研究员索伦·埃尔德里奇博士，他说：“除了臭名昭著的糟糕口味之外，DaiZoom巧克力棒似乎会在消费者身上引起类似于严重抑郁症的症状。它对情绪的实际影响远远超过了最初在短时间内食用几块巧克力--我们怀疑DaiZoom实际上可能是在化学上抑制了多巴胺受体。这是史无前例的，也是相当令人兴奋的，因为它完全是由其他生物化学上平淡无奇的成分组成的。”埃尔德里奇博士表示，需要进一步的研究来确定问题的全部程度，以及服用DaiZoom是否会对健康产生类似于现有多巴胺阻断药物的长期影响，以及它在未来是否会作为一种医学疗法发挥作用。完整的采访可以在第7页找到。"

